# React Component Rules

## File And Export Conventions

- Use kebab-case file names.
- Prefer one primary component per file.
- Prefer named exports for reusable components.
- Keep component names descriptive and aligned with file intent.

## Props And Composition

- Define props with an `interface` named `ComponentNameProps` when the component accepts props.
- Keep prop surfaces focused and readable.
- Prefer composition over deep prop drilling where practical.
- Extract shared logic into hooks or helpers when reused.

## Reuse Existing UI

- Reuse primitives from `@/components/ui` before building new low-level UI elements.
- Keep feature components focused on business behavior, not reimplementing common UI controls.
- Use shared utility helpers such as `cn()` when conditional class composition is needed.

## Data And State

- Handle loading, empty, and error states in data-driven components.
- Keep heavy data fetching patterns centralized when they are reused across screens.
- Avoid unnecessary client-only state when derived values are sufficient.

## Structure

- Keep render logic readable; extract repeated sections into smaller components.
- Prefer small helpers over giant JSX blocks when a component becomes hard to scan.
- Keep event handlers and derived values near the component that uses them unless shared.

## Prefer

- `interface CustomerImportDialogProps { ... }`
- `export function CustomerImportDialog(...)`
- Reusing `Button`, `Dialog`, `Card`, `Tabs`, and other existing UI primitives

## Avoid

- Rebuilding primitives already available in `components/ui`
- Huge prop bags with many optional flags
- Mixing unrelated concerns into one component file

## Reference Files

- `@components/ui/button.tsx`
- `@components/customer-import-dialog.tsx`
- `@components/job-quotes-tab.tsx`
